## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are clear: Pennsylvania's mail-in voting law, as interpreted and mandated by a 2020 Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruling, requires voters to use both an inner secrecy envelope and an outer mailing envelope. Ballots submitted without the inner secrecy envelope are explicitly deemed "naked ballots" and must be rejected. This requirement is a uniform, statewide rule, and official guidance from election officials and materials confirms that ballots not following these instructions will not be counted. No exceptions or curing processes for missing envelopes are documented.

The evidence directly and sufficiently addresses the claim, leaving no essential information lacking for a conclusion.

Therefore, the fact-check finds that the Claim is `supported`. The recorded evidence clearly states that Pennsylvania ballots will be rejected if they are not placed in the required secrecy envelope, which is part of the mandated two-envelope system.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The fact-check confirms that Pennsylvania law mandates a two-envelope system for mail-in ballots, consisting of an inner secrecy envelope and an outer mailing envelope. A [2020 Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruling](https://whyy.org/articles/pa-is-poised-to-take-an-unusually-restrictive-approach-to-mail-in-ballots/) interpreted this law as mandatory, explicitly requiring that ballots submitted without the secrecy envelope—termed "naked ballots"—must be rejected. This is a uniform, statewide rule, and [official election guidance](https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot/pennsylvania/) from the Department of State and local officials emphasizes that ballots must be correctly sealed in both envelopes to be counted, with no documented curing process for this error.